WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.940
Rambo… dead? And what fate did Marvel&nbsp;
really intend for Loki? Here are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.674
of the biggest changes ever made&nbsp;
to movies after a test screening.

00:00:09.674 --> 00:00:13.620
Fatal Attraction remains one of the&nbsp;
most successful thrillers of the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:19.140
a film that launched Glenn Close into stardom and&nbsp;
earned six Academy Award nominations. The film is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.340
still a memorable, tense psychological thrill&nbsp;
ride thanks to the incredible lead performances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.440
of Close and Michael Douglas. But according to&nbsp;
Close it was actually originally going to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.880
in an even darker way than it ultimately did.&nbsp;
Remember the scene in which Dan goes to Alex's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.200
apartment and they fight over a kitchen knife?&nbsp;
Well, Alex was supposed to use that moment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.100
get Dan's prints on the knife — which she&nbsp;
would then kill herself with and use to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.900
frame him for her murder. Test audiences had&nbsp;
something else in mind. Close told Movieline,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.280
"The original ending was a gorgeous&nbsp;
piece of film noir. She kills herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.960
but makes sure that his prints are all over&nbsp;
the knife, and he gets arrested. He knows he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.000
didn't do it, but he's going to jail anyway. But&nbsp;
audiences wanted some kind of cathartic ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.420
so we went back months later and shot&nbsp;
the ending that's in the movie now."

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.160
A different ending, in which Dan and his wife&nbsp;
Beth fight Alex until Beth shoots her, was added.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.440
Test screenings can sway many different aspects&nbsp;
of a film's plot, but it's not often that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.860
hear about a film that comes out of the test&nbsp;
screening process with a different title.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.860
That's exactly what happened to&nbsp;
the last James Bond film of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.600
1980s and Timothy Dalton's second and&nbsp;
final outing as Bond, Licence To Kill.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.000
The film was originally titled Licence Revoked,&nbsp;
because that's exactly what happens in the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.220
Bond becomes a rogue agent as he goes on a quest&nbsp;
for vengeance against a drug kingpin who killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.140
his friend's wife shortly after their wedding, and&nbsp;
M suspends him and revokes his trademark license&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:45.240
to kill. However, when the film was test-screened&nbsp;
for American audiences, they were puzzled by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.580
title, as they thought of "Licence Revoked" as&nbsp;
something that happens to driver's licenses, not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:55.620
cool secret agent's license to kill. So the&nbsp;
title was altered to avoid any confusion.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.000
"You could have had everything."

00:01:57.356 --> 00:01:59.216
"Don't you want to know why?"

00:01:59.813 --> 00:02:04.380
Scott Pilgrim vs. the World and its source&nbsp;
material, the Scott Pilgrim graphic novel series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.360
by Bryan Lee O'Malley, developed simultaneously,&nbsp;
with O'Malley releasing the final volume of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.380
story just weeks before Edgar Wright's film&nbsp;
hit theaters. That meant Wright and company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.760
did not necessarily have a concrete ending from&nbsp;
the source material to go by when they wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.420
the film, and that meant an ending that&nbsp;
created some trouble at test screenings.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.160
The original ending of the film featured Scott&nbsp;
staying with Knives Chau, his girlfriend in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.600
the beginning of the film, rather than being&nbsp;
with Ramona Flowers, but according to Wright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.860
this created arguments during the testing&nbsp;
process. Wright told Entertainment Weekly,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.160
"The way that we'd originally written&nbsp;
it, which wasn't quite right, is we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.300
were trying to do a 'Graduate' or 'Heartbreak&nbsp;
Kid'-style ending. To tell the honest truth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.980
Ellen Wong [who plays Knives] was so lovely and&nbsp;
so charming that it just made me feel horrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.180
even doing that ending. So the ending&nbsp;
we did have was neither here nor there."

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.980
When the test screenings didn't go well,&nbsp;
Wright, O'Malley, and co-writer Michael Bacall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.060
went back and wrote a new ending in which&nbsp;
Knives encourages Scott to be with Ramona.

00:03:00.357 --> 00:03:02.217
"I'm too cool for you anyway."

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:07.620
A 1980s teen romcom classic written by&nbsp;
John Hughes and directed by Howard Deutch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.640
Pretty in Pink is an interesting case of&nbsp;
test screening changes in part because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.180
you can still experience the original&nbsp;
version of the story if you're willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.440
to track down the novelization of the film.&nbsp;
The book, you see, was already written by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.700
time the studio requested a new ending from&nbsp;
Hughes and Deutch following a test screening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.720
response in which audiences clearly picked&nbsp;
a winner in the battle for Andie's heart.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.700
In the film's original ending, Andie actually&nbsp;
did come around to falling for her longtime best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.920
friend Duckie, who'd been in love with her the&nbsp;
whole time, but test audiences wanted Andie to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.720
end up with Blaine, the upper class guy. Because&nbsp;
the novelization is based on the original script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.880
Duckie still gets the girl in the book, but Hughes&nbsp;
and Deutch crafted a new ending in which Blaine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.740
apologizes to Andie and Duckie, feeling they&nbsp;
should be together, and Duckie vouches for him.

00:03:53.286 --> 00:03:56.901
"You were right. He’s not like the others."

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.700
So, if you like that Andie ended up with&nbsp;
Blaine, you have early test audiences to thank.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:08.280
Neil Marshall's brutal subterranean horror flick&nbsp;
The Descent is a film that changed not so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.720
because of traditional test audiences, but because&nbsp;
of the test that was its release in the U.K.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.400
which happened more than a year before the film&nbsp;
finally made its way to the States. The Descent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.500
is a relentless, grimy thrill ride full of gore&nbsp;
and creature thrills, and its U.K. ending drove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.820
its sense of dread and darkness all the way&nbsp;
through to the final moments, as the film ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:31.980
with the reveal that Sarah, the last survivor of&nbsp;
the cave, only dreamed that she'd escaped, and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:36.300
in reality still sitting in the dark, imagining&nbsp;
her dead daughter sitting across from her.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.320
By the time the U.S. release was looming,&nbsp;
audiences had given enough feedback saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.520
they found the ending too bleak, and&nbsp;
distributor Lionsgate asked for a change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:50.000
So Marshall cut the final minute of the film,&nbsp;
allowing Sarah to escape the cave for real.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.720
It might be hard to believe now, but First Blood&nbsp;
was originally supposed to be a one-and-done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.780
affair, because John Rambo was originally supposed&nbsp;
to die. Yes, Sylvester Stallone's tortured Vietnam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:06.060
veteran who became synonymous with action movie&nbsp;
juggernauts in the 1980s was originally going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:11.220
get killed at the end of his first and only movie.&nbsp;
That's what happens in David Morrell's novel on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.420
which the film is based. It's also what happened&nbsp;
in the first version of Ted Kotcheff's film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.960
which he saw as a suicide mission that&nbsp;
would end with Rambo killing himself.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.980
From the moment he joined the production,&nbsp;
Stallone felt the suicide ending for Rambo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:27.180
was too excessive, and pitched an alternate&nbsp;
ending that Kotcheff ultimately shot on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.080
the same day as his original ending. By the&nbsp;
time the film ended up with Orion Pictures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.920
the distributor had the idea to run a&nbsp;
test screening with the original ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.160
just to see what would happen.&nbsp;
Kotcheff told Entertainment Weekly,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.820
"[The test audience] were all unanimous.&nbsp;
They all said, in different words:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.098
'This is the best action film I've ever&nbsp;
seen. But the ending is horrible!'"

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:51.180
Rollerball sounds like the kind of film that&nbsp;
might work well now. Director John McTiernan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:55.260
who helped give birth to the action movie as&nbsp;
we know it, set out to remake a cult classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:59.617
from director Norman Jewison about&nbsp;
an ultra-brutal futuristic sport.

00:05:59.617 --> 00:06:03.374
"The whole world will be watching&nbsp;
because this is your year."

00:06:03.374 --> 00:06:08.220
The 2001 remake of Rollerball seemed to be in&nbsp;
trouble from the beginning, when McTiernan drew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:12.540
internet ire for opting to change the futuristic&nbsp;
setting of the original film and ditching the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:18.600
first script. Then came test screenings in the&nbsp;
spring of 2001. The response was terrible, thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:23.640
in no small part to an extremely negative review&nbsp;
from Ain't It Cool News, and the studio went into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:29.700
damage control mode. The release date was pushed&nbsp;
to 2002, McTiernan conducted two weeks of reshoots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:35.040
to refine certain scenes, and the studio mandated&nbsp;
that the film be cut to make a PG-13 rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.780
hoping younger audiences would respond to it.&nbsp;
This meant all of the nudity and a good deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:45.000
of the violence were toned down. Rollerball was&nbsp;
ultimately a critical and commercial failure.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.800
Sometimes test audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.700
are extremely specific with what they want.&nbsp;
This was the case with The Lovely Bones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:55.200
Peter Jackson's 2009 adaptation of&nbsp;
Alice Sebold's bestselling novel.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.300
Jackson told Reuters that test audiences had a lot&nbsp;
to say about one of the film's key ending points.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:05.280
The specific complaint was regarding the death&nbsp;
of the child killer Mr. Harvey, who is killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.780
as he is in Sebold's novel, when an icicle hits&nbsp;
him on the shoulder and causes him to fall down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:14.820
into a ravine. Audiences were happy to see Harvey&nbsp;
die, but they felt his death was too quick.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.380
So Jackson went back to the cutting room and&nbsp;
added more "suffering." In the final film, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:25.000
see Harvey's full fall to his death, complete with&nbsp;
bone-crunching bounces and facial lacerations.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:29.520
It's now considered one of Martin Scorsese's&nbsp;
best, but members of the original test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:34.560
screening audience for Goodfellas walked out in&nbsp;
disgust. The reactions were instant and visceral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.000
with several people leaving in the opening&nbsp;
moments of the film as Tommy DeVito violently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.920
stabs Billy Batts to death. Studio executives&nbsp;
were rattled. Speaking with Entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:48.420
Weekly in 2019, Scorsese reflected on&nbsp;
the test screening process, saying,

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:51.720
"I'm in the process of making the&nbsp;
film. I screen it for some people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:55.980
they go 'maybe you don't need that,'&nbsp;
and maybe I do things, or maybe not."

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.820
Following the Goodfellas screening,&nbsp;
Scorsese began tweaking the movie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:04.380
edit. Working directly with editor Thelma&nbsp;
Schoonmaker, the two determined precisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:08.820
how many stab wounds the Batts character could&nbsp;
sustain on film before it became gratuitous.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:14.100
Not all screening comment cards resulted in cuts&nbsp;
to the film, however. Scorsese revealed that while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:18.540
the violence was too much for viewers, they loved&nbsp;
Scorsese's real-life mother in the role of Mrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.280
DeVito, Tommy's mother. Their feedback saved the&nbsp;
heartwarming sequence from the cutting room floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:27.257
and the final result is one of the&nbsp;
greatest gangster movies of all time.

00:08:27.257 --> 00:08:29.280
"I settle down almost every night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.707
but in the morning I'm free. I love&nbsp;
you, Mom. I want to be with you."

00:08:32.707 --> 00:08:37.680
Anchorman: The Legend of Ron Burgundy has become&nbsp;
a modern comedy classic, but the original plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:43.260
was received so poorly by test audiences that it&nbsp;
had to be completely reshot. The retooling left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:48.060
several big name comic actors on the cutting&nbsp;
room floor, like Maya Rudolph, Stephen Root,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:53.580
Amy Poehler and Justin Long. There was even a role&nbsp;
for rap legend Chuck D that had to be abandoned.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:58.500
Will Ferrell superfans need not fret, however,&nbsp;
as all the unused footage from the feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:03.420
film — mostly involving a terrorist group called&nbsp;
The Alarm Clock — was repurposed into a so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:08.640
"lost film" called Wake Up, Ron Burgundy.&nbsp;
Initially released as its own direct-to-DVD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:13.320
feature, it's now included as an extra on&nbsp;
the "Unrated Rich Mahogany Edition" Blu-ray.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:18.900
The theatrical release, of course, became a&nbsp;
huge hit. Anchorman earned over $90 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.640
at the box office and has a rating of&nbsp;
86% with audiences on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:28.134
but beyond the numbers it became what its&nbsp;
titular character is known as: a legend.

00:09:28.294 --> 00:09:32.914
"If you want to throw down, it's fine. I've got&nbsp;
Jack Johnson and Tom O'Leary waiting for you."

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:38.940
Thor may be the star of his own series of movies&nbsp;
in the MCU, but Loki is just as beloved. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:42.840
Loki is easily the most popular&nbsp;
villain-slash-antihero of the multiverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:48.420
with a redemption arc in Thor: Ragnarok and his&nbsp;
own extremely popular series on Disney+. Imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:52.800
then, if Loki had truly died at the end of&nbsp;
Thor: The Dark World as originally intended.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.820
Tom Hiddleston revealed on the Empire&nbsp;
film podcast that Loki's death in Thor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:02.580
The Dark World was meant to stick, but that test&nbsp;
screening audiences rejected the idea. Naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:06.900
it makes sense that fans wouldn't trust any&nbsp;
Loki death scene, as the notorious trickster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:11.400
character is known for his misleading illusions.&nbsp;
Indeed, moments earlier in Thor: The Dark World,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:15.660
the character makes it look like he cuts off his&nbsp;
brother's hand in a moment of betrayal. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:19.740
Loki's no stranger to faking his death,&nbsp;
as he does at the end of the first Thor.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:24.900
It's therefore fitting that the sly adopted&nbsp;
Asgardian pulls another fast one. Loki's The Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:29.340
World death was meant to serve as a redemption arc&nbsp;
for the mischievous villain. While it can be said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:33.360
that undermining that intention in The Dark World&nbsp;
leaves the middle of the film with no real stakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.440
it ultimately allows Loki to serve&nbsp;
a much greater purpose — and enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:41.280
a much more satisfying redemption —&nbsp;
in Thor: Ragnarok and beyond.

